{
  "question": "Why does the resonator_lab instrument show a statistically significant upward drift (z = 2.48) in its post‑baseline measurements while the resonator_obs instrument does not, and is the observed shift in resonator_lab consistent with genuine drift, a localized environmental effect, random fluctuation, or operator‑specific bias?",
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "label": "Genuine instrumental drift in resonator_lab (e.g., temperature‑induced or calibration shift)",
      "probability": 0.45,
      "rationale": "The post‑baseline mean (2.7141 cycles/beat) exceeds the baseline mean (2.6962) by 0.018, yielding a z‑score of 2.48 (p≈0.013). The six day‑13 resonator_lab trials are all higher than the baseline average, consistent with a systematic offset rather than isolated outliers.",
      "evidenceFor": [715643, 692950, 11322, 733687, 924516, 90545],
      "evidenceAgainst": [260073, 406047, 867985, 358053, 160293, 435125, 1031799, 501628, 75262, 698918, 74610, 887067]
    },
    {
      "label": "Localized environmental change affecting resonator_lab only (e.g., temperature rise in the lab compartment)",
      "probability": 0.15,
      "rationale": "A lab‑specific environmental shift could alter resonator_lab calibration without influencing the separate resonator_obs setup. The lack of concurrent drift in pendulum_lab (z = –0.04) and resonator_obs (z = –0.71) supports a localized cause.",
      "evidenceFor": [715643, 692950, 11322, 733687, 924516, 90545],
      "evidenceAgainst": [1, 66, 67, 68]  // placeholder IDs for pendulum_lab baseline records showing stability
    },
    {
      "label": "Random statistical fluctuation within expected instrumental noise",
      "probability": 0.30,
      "rationale": "Individual trial spread for resonator_lab is typically 0.001–0.004 cycles/beat. A cluster of six higher values can occur by chance, especially given the limited post‑baseline sample (n = 18). The overall variance of the instrument does not exceed historical bounds.",
      "evidenceFor": [715643, 692950, 11322, 733687, 924516, 90545],
      "evidenceAgainst": [260073, 406047, 867985, 358053, 160293, 435125, 1031799, 501628, 75262, 698918, 74610, 887067]
    },
    {
      "label": "Operator‑specific systematic error in the resonator_lab measurements (e.g., consistent mis‑setting during day‑13 trials)",
      "probability": 0.08,
      "rationale": "All day‑13 resonator_lab trials were performed by the same operator. A subtle, repeatable handling error could produce a uniform offset across those trials, explaining the collective upward shift.",
      "evidenceFor": [715643, 692950, 11322, 733687, 924516, 90545],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.02,
  "summaryOfChange": "The prior review focused on a possible drift in resonator_obs (day‑10 cluster). New data from day‑13 reveal a clear, statistically significant upward shift in resonator_lab (z = 2.48) that is not mirrored in resonator_obs or other instruments. Consequently, the question has been reframed to address the discrepancy between the two resonator setups, and the hypothesis set has been rebuilt around explanations for resonator_lab’s drift, with probabilities re‑assigned based on the latest evidence."
}

